ary decides  that this is a serious alert it goes to  the president you probably have all  heard now about the nuclear football  which is this suitcase carried by the  gentlemen here in the middle of the  screen there's somebody with the  president  24/7 I assume they're not actually you  know I mean anyway with the president  24/7 you can figure that out  ah and the reason is to receive this  alert in this black suitcase is a book  of options so there will be an indicate  you know there the advisors might say we  recommend option 27 but the OP you know  there are lots of options or maybe you  might think of option 54 and then you  can look at what those options are how  many missiles go where you know in some  cases I think it tells you how many  people would die does not tell you what  the consequences but climatic  consequences of such an attack would be  so the president has about ten minutes  to make this decision before he or she  has to tell the Pentagon that yes I want  to launch these missiles okay  now sometimes the sensors are wrong  sometimes other things go wrong and  there have been in the past several  decades nuclear near misses I'm going to  touch on some of the ones we know about  I'm guessing we don't know about all of  them these are the ones that are maybe  the most famous so here's a situation in  1979 and the u.s. received indication  that it was under a full-scale attack it  looked like just the kind of attack they  would be expecting from the Soviet Union  and up goes the alert level and they  figure out that the reason it looks like  an attack because somebody has put a  training tape in the computer and the  training tape is an attack so it looks  just like what they were expecting and  had been training for it did not rise to  the level of the president here's a case  in the Soviet Union in 1983 the  satellites the Soviet satellites detect  what looked to be several incoming  missiles the person in charge that night  in the Soviet command center everything  checked out it looked like a real you  know like there was no corruption of the  data and protocol calls for him to tell  his supervisor and then it would have  gone up from there and based on a gut  feeling he disobeyed the rules and and  decided said oh it's a false alarm  even though he didn't know it's a false  alarm it was a false alarm but he didn't  know  and he was later reprimanded there's a  movie about him the man who saved the  world okay in 95 this is now Russia and  not the Soviet Union um  Norway launched a small what's called a  sounding rocket it was going to do some  scientific experiments in the atmosphere  and its trajectory looked like the  trajectory of a u.s. submarine launched  missile off the coast of Russia that  might have been designed to destroy  Russia's early warning radars as a  prelude to a larger attack and this  warning did apparently go up to Yeltsin  there's a dispute about whether or not  that actually whether it went that far  up Yeltsin says it did people other than  Yeltsin say it didn't it's hard to  imagine the president could be that  confused  right okay so those are cases from the  past what about now this is a quote from  a general Cartwright who used to be the  commander of all the US nuclear weapons  I just let you read it but he's making  the point that cyber attacks are a new  unknown we don't really know how to  factor them in but clearly it's not  going to make things better now our new  Secretary of Defense before he became  Secretary of Defense says something very  interesting in Senate testimony last  year he said is it time to reduce the  Triad to a dyad removing the land-based  missiles this would reduce the false  alarm danger so unfortunately when he  was uh in his confirmation hearings  he was really pounded on this and ended  up saying oh no I believe in the triad  but clearly I think this reflects his  his his own assessment now I don't you  know I don't necessarily think he's  going to act on it but it's a good sign  so now I'm going to talk about the  second thing that I mentioned which is  using nuclear weapons first so a lot of  people think that the US would use that  at the point of US nuclear weapons is to  deter deter what  well deter a nuclear attack on the  United States deter a nuclear attack on  our allies turns out that it's it's more  complicated okay so our previous speaker  said pointed out that in fact you have  had use nuclear weapons first in  Hiroshima and Nagasaki and our policy  has been  we reserve the right to use nuclear  weapons first under the this is the  second Bush administration they they did  a Nuclear Posture review where they  looked at US policy it was leaked and  this is what it says the u.s. reserves  the right to use nuclear weapons first  against any country in response to an  attack with conventional weapons or with  chemical or biological weapons and it  specifically lifted several countries  okay Russia China North Korea Iran who  you know there were no questions about  Iranian nuclear program at that point  Iraq Syria and Libya now what's  interesting is that this policy first  used really flourished during the Cold  War when the US and NATO thought that  Russia had conventional superior the  Soviet Union had conventional  superiority might roll into Western  Europe and that we needed nuclear  weapons to be able to deter that and in  fact at one point the US had 7000  tactical nuclear weapons in Europe okay  now there are only 200 all right so the  policy evolved under the Obama  administration their Nuclear Posture  review which is not classified you can  find it online  says that the primary purpose not the  only but the primary purpose is to deter  the use of nuclear weapons and the US  will not use nuclear weapons against  countries that don't have them so in  other words the US would not use them  against Iraq or Syria or Libya okay so  we take those off the list and in fact  the list it didn't name these countries  but you can figure it out yourself  it would only use them against countries  that have nuclear weapons now there are  only a few that  we whatever attack and they are Russia  China and North Korea  moreover the goal is to have a no first  youth policy got it now President Obama  so this is in 2010 in the last few  months of his administration there was  serious consideration of adopting a no  first use policy Obama was very eager to  do so actually and one of the reasons he  didn't is he got tremendous pushback  from Secretary of Defense ash Carter  Secretary of State John Kerry and  Secretary of Energy Moniz who argued  that this would send the wrong message  to Russia there were tensions with  Russia so we lost that opportunity for  you know who knows how long so let me  move on to presidential launch Authority  now it is the president who has the sole  authority to make a decision that the US  will use its nuclear weapons and I think  Jo talked about this situation earlier  but there is a scary a historical event  in which President Nixon noted correctly  I can go back into my office and pick up  the telephone and in 25 minutes 70  million people will be dead okay he had  the authority to do that  and during Watergate I didn't know about  the talking to the portraits but he was  definitely drinking heavily in a bad way  and Schlesinger told the chiefs of staff  if you get any orders come talk to me  first and that was not legal okay he had  no authority to do that it's unclear if  had Nixon tried to order an attack what  would have happened  okay now let's talk about constraining  presidential launch Authority ah and I  would I would say that we're going to  divide it into two parts nuclear  retaliation  now if so as long as you have this  launch on warning option as long as you  have our missiles on hair-trigger alert  there are only about 10 minutes to make  a decision about launching those weapons  it's totally infeasible that you could  include more than one person in making  that decision okay so as long as we have  that policy we are stuck with this one  person making that decision that's  another reason I think to get rid of  hair trigger alert for our land-based  missiles and launch on warning options  now presumably if there was a decision  to use nuclear weapons first it would  not be you know you would have more than  10 minutes to make that decision and  that opens up the door to constraining  presidential authority in that case  nuclear first use and we've already  heard there is legislation restricting  the first use of nuclear weapons act  sponsored by Senator Markey our own  senator and representative Liu one  minute and it would prohibit the  president from launching a first strike  without congressional declaration of war  and there was a press conference just  two days ago you can see senator Markey  there and representative Liu and a  number of groups including Peace Action  and Union of Concerned Scientists  delivered 500,000 petitions now on let's  be clear this legislation will not pass  will not even be voted on but it is a  useful  it means to have this discussion so  useful means to get people to talk to  their office their representatives and  their Senate offices about this and and  we go from there okay we have a lot of  things that we need to change US policy  on we want to remove hair triggers for  land-based nuclear weapons or get rid of  the land-based nuclear weapons as mattis  suggested we and that will open up the  door to involving other people in a  decision to use weapons in response to  an attack you know even if there were an  attack you don't have to respond right  away unless you're worried about losing  your land-based missiles okay then we  have the issue of first youth options I  wouldn't say it's that we have a first  use policy but we have a policy of  retaining first youth options that's a  problem  and then there's the problem of  presidential authority so I just want to  take complicated a little bit and  explain to you that we have more  problems than you thought we did and  thank you I should say one more thing we  have on our website and I think there'll  be a few copies out there a very short  report on reducing the risk of nuclear  war you can get in on our website if  there aren't copies out there and that's  it  [Applause]  [Music]

